    "result_text": "The full suite (a single acceptance test class) passes: **BUILD SUCCESS**, 1 test run, 0 failures.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country E.164 national-number length validation in the owner-create path.\n\n**Change** \u2014 `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`:\n\n1. Added a `hasValidNationalLength(String e164)` helper that, given the canonical `+`-prefixed digit string produced by `toE164`, checks the national-number length against the country code: `+61` (Australia) requires **9** national digits, `+1` (NANP) requires **10**. Other country codes are left to the existing general 8\u201315 digit rule.\n\n2. Wired it into `addOwner`, so a telephone whose national length is wrong for its country is rejected with **400 Bad Request**:\n   ```java\n   String telephone = toE164(owner.getTelephone());\n   if (telephone == null || !hasValidNationalLength(telephone)) {\n       return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST);\n   }\n   ```\n\nI deliberately kept the check out of `toE164` itself so the duplicate-detection loop (which normalizes existing/seed owners, some of whom carry 10-digit national numbers under the assumed `+61`) keeps its existing behavior \u2014 the new rule only gates the incoming create payload.\n\nThe new `errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` test (`+61 123` \u2192 400) passes, and the fully-valid `uniqueTelephone()` payloads (`04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 `+61` with 9 national digits) still succeed.",
